# Fernpage MCP tool catalog

This is the tool surface that AI agents use to build and maintain Fernpage sites. `docs/brief.md` wins over this document. If they disagree, fix this document.

Status: **draft, written while the owner was away.** I made the calls I had to and marked each one. They are all listed under [Assumptions](#assumptions) and [Open questions](#open-questions) at the end. The owner should review those first.

## Server

- Endpoint: `https://mcp.<domain>/mcp`. This is its own protocol app (`apps/mcp`, Hono plus the official MCP SDK). The product domain isn't decided yet.
- Transport: stateless, buffered JSON responses. No streaming or subscription tools. Agents don't need live pushes: they read, act, and read again.
- Auth: standard MCP OAuth against the auth provider. During consent the user picks one workspace, and the token acts in that workspace. To act in a different workspace, the user authorizes again. It's the same resource, not a second server.
- Catalog: every caller sees the same static list of tools. The server checks authorization on every call. A call it refuses gets a typed error, never a hidden tool.
- Every tool calls the same product capabilities that the browser app and the visitor runtime SDK use. Writes made through MCP land in the same reactive database, so every open page sees saved-data changes live.

## Conventions

**Naming.** Tool names are `verb_noun` in snake_case. The noun is plural when the tool takes a list (`create_files`), and singular for single-item reads (`read_file`, `get_entry`). Collection reads use `list_`.

**Identity.**

| Thing | Identity | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Workspace | `workspace_id`, 12-character Base58 | Stable, opaque. |
| Site | `site_id`, 12-character Base58 | Stable, opaque. Names and titles are never accepted where something gets destroyed. |
| File | `site_id` + `path` | The path *is* the file's identity, because it is the file's public URL. Paths are relative (`index.html`, `img/cake.jpg`), case-sensitive, `/`-separated, with no leading `/`, no `..`, and at most 512 bytes. The `_fernpage/` prefix is reserved. |
| Collection | `site_id` + `collection` name | Declared in the site's data rules. Names match `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,39}$`. |
| Entry | `site_id` + `collection` + `entry_id` | The server generates a 12-character Base58 ID, unless the caller supplied a key when creating the entry (see `create_entries`). |

Reads may resolve by name. For example, `list_sites` takes a `search` string, and its results carry stable IDs. Every write and delete takes stable IDs.

**Revisions and concurrency.** Files, entries, and the data-rules document each carry an opaque `revision` string. A tool that replaces or patches existing state takes `expected_revision`. If the state has moved on, the tool refuses with `revision_conflict` and returns the current revision. For entries it also returns the current value, so the agent can re-apply its change without another read. Visitors may be writing saved data at the same moment an agent is, so entry updates always make the agent choose (see `update_entries`).

**Batches.** A tool that takes a list runs the product's single-item operation once per item. Each item gets its own authorization, validation, and quota check. Results come back one per item, in input order, typed by `status`, with failures inline. Successes are never rolled back because a sibling failed. The one exception is `create_site`, which is all-or-nothing (see below). If authorization is refused before anything lands, the whole call fails with the re-auth challenge.

```ts
type ToolError = {
  code: ErrorCode;
  message: string;            // product copy: says what failed and the next move
  [detail: string]: unknown;  // measured facts, e.g. { size_bytes, limit_bytes }
};
type Failed = { status: "failed"; error: ToolError };
```

**Annotations.** These follow MCP `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `idempotentHint`, and `openWorldHint`, and they describe the tool's worst possible effect. A tool is `openWorldHint: true` when it can fetch a caller-supplied URL. Annotations are hints for the host. The server's authorization gate is what enforces access.

**No confirmation arguments.** Nothing asks for `confirm: true`. Approval belongs to the host, and authority belongs to the server. Every tool is safe to run unattended.

**File content input.** When a tool accepts file bytes, they come in a tagged `source`:

```ts
type FileSource =
  | { type: "text"; text: string }        // UTF-8 text: HTML, CSS, JS, JSON, SVG, ...
  | { type: "url"; url: string }          // primary path for images, fonts, any binary or large file
  | { type: "base64"; data: string };     // tiny binaries only, ≤ 64 KB decoded
```

Prefer `url` for anything that isn't text. The server fetches it with SSRF protections: HTTPS only, no private or link-local addresses, a 15 s timeout, and the per-file size cap. The server sets `content_type` from the file extension.

## Shared resource schemas

These are the same shapes the HTTP API and the browser app use.

```ts
type Workspace = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  role: "owner" | "member";
};

type Site = {
  id: string;
  workspace_id: string;
  url: string;              // public address, minted by the server; never build it yourself
  app_url: string;          // the site in the browser app (where Trash restore lives)
  status: "live" | "trashed";
  purge_at: string | null;  // ISO time; set only while trashed
  // Derived from index.html on every read, never stored separately:
  title: string;            // <title>, else "Untitled site"
  description: string | null;  // <meta name="description">
  favicon_path: string | null; // <link rel="icon"> href, if it resolves to a site file
  file_count: number;
  total_bytes: number;
  data: {
    rules_revision: string;
    collections: Array<{ name: string; entry_count: number; bytes: number }>;
    total_bytes: number;
  };
  created_at: string;
  updated_at: string;
};

type FileInfo = {
  path: string;
  url: string;              // public URL of this file
  content_type: string;
  size_bytes: number;
  revision: string;         // changes whenever the content changes
  updated_at: string;
};

type Entry = {
  id: string;
  collection: string;
  value: Record<string, JsonValue>;  // a JSON object, ≤ 32 KB serialized
  created_by: Author;
  updated_by: Author;
  revision: string;
  created_at: string;
  updated_at: string;
};

type Author =
  | { kind: "visitor"; visitor_id: string }                      // anonymous browser identity from the runtime SDK
  | { kind: "member"; user_id: string; via: "app" | "agent" };   // a workspace member, directly or through an agent

type DataRules = {
  revision: string;
  collections: Record<string, CollectionRules>;
};

type CollectionRules = {
  read:   Access;   // who may read entries
  create: Access;   // who may add entries
  update: Access;   // who may change an entry
  delete: Access;   // who may remove an entry
};

// Applies to visitors. Workspace members, and agents acting for them, may always do everything.
type Access =
  | "anyone"    // any visitor
  | "creator"   // only the visitor who created the entry (for create: same as "anyone")
  | "owner";    // no visitor; workspace members only
```

The brief's two examples, written as rules:

```json
{
  "collections": {
    "checklist": { "read": "anyone", "create": "owner", "update": "anyone",  "delete": "owner" },
    "rsvps":     { "read": "creator", "create": "anyone", "update": "creator", "delete": "creator" },
    "settings":  { "read": "anyone", "create": "owner", "update": "owner",   "delete": "owner" }
  }
}
```

