## Workspaces

### `list_workspaces`

> List the Fernpage workspaces this connection can create and manage sites in.

```ts
input:  {}
output: { workspaces: Workspace[] }
```

In practice this returns the one workspace chosen at consent. The tool exists so agents can discover the `workspace_id` instead of guessing it, and so the catalog keeps working if one connection is later allowed into several workspaces.

## Sites

### `list_sites`

> List and search Fernpage sites in a workspace by title.

```ts
input: {
  workspace_id: string;
  search?: string;    // case-insensitive match on title; narrows only
  cursor?: string;
  limit?: number;     // default 25, max 100
}
output: { sites: Site[]; next_cursor: string | null }
```

Trashed sites are excluded. When several sites match a search, the agent picks one by `id`.

### `get_site`

> Get a Fernpage site's URL, title, description, favicon, file totals, and saved-data summary.

```ts
input:  { site_id: string }
output: { site: Site }
```

Title, description, and favicon come from `index.html` every time the site is read. To change them, edit `index.html`. No tool sets them directly. A trashed site still resolves here, with `status: "trashed"` and `purge_at`, so an agent can explain what happened and point the user to `app_url`.

### `create_site`

> Create and publish a new Fernpage site in a workspace from its index.html and any other starting files.

```ts
input: {
  workspace_id: string;
  files: Array<{ path: string; source: FileSource }>;  // 1–50 items; must include "index.html"
}
output: { site: Site; files: FileInfo[] }
```

- One `index.html` is enough. The site goes live at `site.url` as soon as the call returns.
- **All or nothing.** If any file fails (bad path, fetch failure, too large), no site is created, and the error lists every failing item. A half-built new site has no value. Add more files afterwards with `create_files`.
- Not idempotent: every successful call makes a new site. After a timeout or an unknown outcome, check `list_sites` before retrying.
- Errors: `index_required`, `invalid_path`, `duplicate_path`, `too_large`, `fetch_failed`, `quota_exceeded`, `forbidden`.

### `delete_site`

> Delete a Fernpage site by moving it to Trash, where it can be restored in the browser app for 30 days.

```ts
input:  { site_id: string }
output: { status: "trashed"; site_id: string; purge_at: string; app_url: string }
```

- While a site is in Trash, its URL stops serving, and its files and saved data are kept untouched. After 30 days, both are permanently purged by a bounded, resumable deletion job.
- **There is no restore tool.** Restoring happens only in the browser app. The result includes `app_url` so the agent can tell the user where to go.
- Calling it on a site that's already trashed returns the same result (idempotent).
- Any other tool called on a trashed site fails with `site_in_trash`, which includes `app_url`.

## Files

### `list_files`

> List a Fernpage site's files with their paths, sizes, content types, and revisions.

```ts
input: {
  site_id: string;
  prefix?: string;    // e.g. "img/"; narrows only
  cursor?: string;
  limit?: number;     // default 200, max 1000
}
output: { files: FileInfo[]; next_cursor: string | null }
```

This is the cheap way to get the `revision` values that `replace_files` needs.

### `read_file`

> Read one file from a Fernpage site: text content inline, or a download link for binary files.

```ts
input:  { site_id: string; path: string }
output:
  | { kind: "text";   file: FileInfo; text: string }                        // text files ≤ 512 KB
  | { kind: "binary"; file: FileInfo; download_url: string; expires_at: string }
```

This tool reads one file at a time on purpose, so a single call can't flood the agent's context. A text file over 512 KB fails with `too_large` and reports its `size_bytes`.

### `create_files`

> Add new files to a Fernpage site; refuses any path that already exists.

```ts
input: {
  site_id: string;
  files: Array<{ path: string; source: FileSource }>;  // 1–50 items
}
output: {
  results: Array<
    | { status: "created"; path: string; file: FileInfo }
    | ({ path: string } & Failed)   // already_exists, invalid_path, too_large, fetch_failed, quota_exceeded
  >;
}
```

Because it never overwrites, an agent that thinks it's adding a file can't destroy one by accident. `already_exists` returns the existing file's `revision` and names `replace_files` as the next step.

### `replace_files`

> Replace the whole content of existing files on a Fernpage site.

```ts
input: {
  site_id: string;
  files: Array<{
    path: string;
    source: FileSource;
    expected_revision: string;   // required; get it from list_files or a previous write
  }>;                            // 1–50 items
}
output: {
  results: Array<
    | { status: "replaced"; path: string; file: FileInfo }
    | { status: "unchanged"; path: string; file: FileInfo }   // new content was byte-identical
    | ({ path: string } & Failed)  // not_found, revision_conflict, too_large, fetch_failed, quota_exceeded
  >;
}
```

- Refuses paths that don't exist (`not_found`, which names `create_files`). It never creates a file by accident.
- `expected_revision` is required so the agent never silently overwrites a change made in the browser app, or by another agent, since its last read.
- Replacing `index.html` updates the site's title, description, and favicon.

### `edit_file`

> Edit one text file on a Fernpage site by replacing exact snippets, for small changes without resending the file.

```ts
input: {
  site_id: string;
  path: string;
  edits: Array<{
    find: string;      // exact text; must occur exactly once in the file at apply time
    replace: string;
  }>;                  // 1–20 edits, applied in order, all or nothing
}
output: { status: "edited"; file: FileInfo; applied: number }
```

- This is the tool for the brief's "often one small edit". The `find` anchors check themselves, so the tool needs no `expected_revision`. If the file changed so a snippet no longer matches, the edit fails cleanly.
- Errors: `not_found`, `not_text`, `find_not_found` (with the edit index), `find_ambiguous` (with the edit index and match count), `too_large`.

### `delete_files`

> Remove files from a Fernpage site.

```ts
input: {
  site_id: string;
  paths: string[];     // 1–100 items
}
output: {
  results: Array<
    | { status: "deleted"; path: string }
    | ({ path: string } & Failed)   // not_found, index_required
  >;
}
```

- `index.html` can't be deleted (`index_required`): a site always has one. To change it, use `replace_files` or `edit_file`. To remove the whole site, use `delete_site`.
- A path that doesn't exist is reported as `not_found`, so typos get noticed instead of passing silently.
- File deletion is permanent. Only whole sites go to Trash.

## Saved data

Agents get the same entry-by-entry view of saved data that visitors get. The difference is permissions: an agent acts for a workspace member, so data rules never limit it. Each agent write is its own transaction, with nothing like an "edit the whole dataset" operation. That way visitors writing at the same moment are never clobbered wholesale, and every open page updates live, entry by entry.

### `get_data_rules`

> Get the rules that decide which visitors may read, create, update, or delete each saved-data collection on a Fernpage site.

```ts
input:  { site_id: string }
output: { rules: DataRules }
```

### `set_data_rules`

> Replace a Fernpage site's saved-data rules, declaring its collections and who may write them.

```ts
input: {
  site_id: string;
  collections: Record<string, CollectionRules>;   // the complete new rule set
  expected_revision: string;                      // from get_data_rules or get_site
}
output: { rules: DataRules }
```

- Declaring a collection here is how it comes into existence. Entries can only live in declared collections.
- **Leaving out a collection that still has entries fails** with `collection_has_entries` (which includes the counts). Rules never delete data implicitly. Empty the collection with `delete_all_entries` first.
- Changes apply to visitor requests at once.
- Errors: `revision_conflict`, `invalid_rules`, `collection_has_entries`.

### `list_entries`

> List or search saved-data entries in a Fernpage site's collection, such as RSVPs or checklist items.

```ts
input: {
  site_id: string;
  collection: string;
  search?: string;            // case-insensitive substring over string values; narrows only
  created_by?: "visitor" | "member";
  cursor?: string;
  limit?: number;             // default 50, max 200
}
output: { entries: Entry[]; next_cursor: string | null; total: number }
```

Entries are ordered by `created_at`, oldest first. `search` is how an agent finds "the RSVP with the misspelled name" without paging through everything.

### `get_entry`

> Get one saved-data entry from a Fernpage site by its ID.

```ts
input:  { site_id: string; collection: string; entry_id: string }
output: { entry: Entry }
```

### `create_entries`

> Add saved-data entries to a Fernpage site's collection, for example to seed a checklist or initial settings.

```ts
input: {
  site_id: string;
  collection: string;
  entries: Array<{
    key?: string;   // caller-chosen ID, ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}$, e.g. "main" for a settings entry
    value: Record<string, JsonValue>;
  }>;               // 1–100 items
}
output: {
  results: Array<
    | { status: "created"; entry: Entry }
    | ({ index: number; key?: string } & Failed)  // already_exists, too_large, collection_not_declared, quota_exceeded
  >;
}
```

- If `key` is left out, the server generates an ID. A `key` that is already taken fails with `already_exists`. It never overwrites the existing entry, and the error names `update_entries`.
- Fixed keys exist for settings-style data: the site's JavaScript can read `settings/main` directly without searching for it.
- `collection_not_declared` names `set_data_rules` as the next step.

### `update_entries`

> Change fields of existing saved-data entries on a Fernpage site, such as correcting a misspelled RSVP name or changing a setting.

```ts
input: {
  site_id: string;
  collection: string;
  updates: Array<{
    entry_id: string;
    patch: Record<string, JsonValue>;   // JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396): listed fields set, null removes a field
    expected_revision: string | null;   // required: a revision, or null to apply on top of whatever is current
  }>;                                   // 1–100 items
}
output: {
  results: Array<
    | { status: "updated"; entry: Entry }
    | { status: "unchanged"; entry: Entry }
    | ({ entry_id: string } & Failed)   // not_found, revision_conflict (with current entry), too_large
  >;
}
```

- **`expected_revision` is a required field.** The agent must decide, for each entry, how to treat visitor writes that happen concurrently:
  - Pass a revision when the change depends on what the agent read, such as rewriting a name it looked at. If a visitor edited the entry in between, the item fails with `revision_conflict` and returns the current entry, so the agent can re-check and retry.
  - Pass `null` for a change that stands on its own, such as setting `settings/main.rsvp_deadline`. The merge patch touches only the listed fields, so a visitor ticking a different field of the same entry is kept.
- `updated_by` records `{ kind: "member", via: "agent" }`. `created_by` never changes, so an RSVP the agent corrects still belongs to the visitor who created it.

### `delete_entries`

> Remove specific saved-data entries from a Fernpage site's collection by ID, such as test RSVPs.

```ts
input: {
  site_id: string;
  collection: string;
  entry_ids: string[];   // 1–100 items
}
output: {
  results: Array<
    | { status: "deleted"; entry_id: string }
    | ({ entry_id: string } & Failed)   // not_found
  >;
}
```

Entry deletion is permanent and appears live on every open page.

### `delete_all_entries`

> Delete every saved-data entry in one collection of a Fernpage site, such as clearing test data before launch.

```ts
input:  { site_id: string; collection: string }
output: { status: "emptied"; collection: string; deleted: number; kept_concurrent: number }
```

- Deletes the entries that existed when the call started. Entries that visitors create while it runs are kept and counted in `kept_concurrent`, so a real RSVP that arrives mid-clear isn't lost.
- The collection and its rules stay. Only entries are removed.
- This is a separate tool, rather than an option on `delete_entries`, so leaving out an argument can never turn "delete these" into "delete everything".
- Permanent. Calling it on an already-empty collection returns `deleted: 0`.

